1. INTRODUCTION: WHY RUNTIME SEMANTICS MATTER

1.1 The Gap

A3 §7 sketches failure semantics. A1 §6.3 sketches sub-task termination. But neither provides the full picture:

Question Answered by
What is a sub-task, exactly? A3 §6
How is it scheduled? A1 §5.1
How is it verified? A5 §4
How is it logged? A7 §5.2
What happens when it fails? A8
How does the next sub-task begin? A8
When does the plan abort? A8
How does recovery work? A8

A8 closes the gap. It specifies the runtime layer that executes plans.

1.2 The Two Obligations

A8 has two load-bearing obligations to the Verifiable Agency Theorem:

Obligation O1 (Bounded Composition). Every composition operator produces a bounded sub-task sequence.

Obligation O2 (Bounded Recovery). Every recovery path (retry, rollback, escalation) is bounded in cycles and depth.

If O1 fails, DECOMPOSE can produce unbounded sequences, violating the finiteness of the behavior set.
If O2 fails, recovery can run forever, violating T-AET.

1.3 The Design Principle

Composition is compile-time. Recovery is bounded. Failure is always logged. Escalation is always available. Improvisation is never permitted.

