4. THE COMPOSITION OPERATORS

4.1 Sequential (;)

Definition 5 (Sequential). p1 ; p2 — execute p1 to completion, then p2.

Sub-task sequence: DECOMPOSE(p1) ‖ DECOMPOSE(p2) (concatenation).

Length: |DECOMPOSE(p1)| + |DECOMPOSE(p2)|.

Failure: If p1 fails, p2 does not execute. The composite plan fails with p1's failure reason.

4.2 Conditional (if)

Definition 6 (Conditional). if predicate then p1 else p2.

Sub-task sequence:

```
⟨ EVALUATE(predicate), then_branch ‖ DECOMPOSE(p1) if true, else_branch ‖ DECOMPOSE(p2) ⟩
```

Length: 1 + max(|DECOMPOSE(p1)|, |DECOMPOSE(p2)|).

Failure: If predicate evaluation fails, the conditional fails. If the selected branch fails, the conditional fails.

4.3 Bounded Iteration (while_bounded)

Definition 7 (Bounded Iteration). while_bounded predicate max_n p.

Sub-task sequence: Up to max_n iterations of ⟨ EVALUATE(predicate), DECOMPOSE(p) ⟩.

Length: max_n × (1 + |DECOMPOSE(p)|).

Termination: Iteration stops when predicate is false OR max_n iterations completed, whichever comes first.

Failure: If predicate evaluation fails, iteration halts. If a body execution fails, the iteration fails (subject to the body's failure policy).

4.4 Bounded PlanStep Invocation (PlanStep)

Definition 8 (PlanStep Invocation). PlanStep(p') — invoke sub-plan p'.

Sub-task sequence: DECOMPOSE(p').

Depth: Bounded by D_invoke (A6 §2.4).

Failure: Handled by A6 §6 (invocation failure).

4.5 The Composition Boundedness Property

Proposition A8-C (Composition Boundedness). Every composition operator produces a sub-task sequence of length ≤ K × D_invoke.

Proof. By structural induction over A3 §2.2 step kinds.

· ActionStep: length 1. 1 ≤ K × D_invoke. ✓
· PlanStep: bounded by A6 §2.4 (DAG depth D_invoke). ✓
· ConditionalStep: length 1 + max(branches). Both branches are bounded by induction. ✓

By A3 §5.3 (composition preserves |p| ≤ K), the composite length is bounded. □

