6. RETRY SEMANTICS

6.1 Retry Definition

Definition 10 (Retry). A retry re-executes a sub-task after a failure. Retries are counted by attempt (Definition 1).

6.2 Retry Bounds

Rule R1 (Attempt Bound). attempt ≤ max_attempts, a declared per-plan constant.

Rule R2 (Total Cycles Bound). Total cycles across all attempts of all sub-tasks must not exceed T-AET.

Rule R3 (No Infinite Retry). If attempt = max_attempts and the sub-task still fails, the retry policy is exhausted. The plan then applies its declared failure policy (Halt, Rollback, etc.) without further retry.

6.3 Determinism of Retry

Proposition A8-R (Retry Determinism). For a deterministic sub-task, retries produce identical outcomes.

Proof. By Corollary 5 (Determinism of Behavior), the same sub-task on the same state produces the same result. Retries do not change the state between attempts (Rule F1 §5.2, retry does not modify state). Therefore retries are deterministic. □

Corollary A8-R1 (Retry Futility Detection). If a sub-task fails deterministically, retry will also fail. The retry policy is only useful if the underlying failure was caused by an environmental change (sensor update, external event).

Note. This corollary is important. It says: retries are only useful for environmental recovery, not for computation recovery. Deployments must account for this when declaring retry policies.

