7. ROLLBACK SEMANTICS

7.1 Rollback Definition

Definition 11 (Rollback). Rollback restores the state to a declared checkpoint recorded in the AOL (A7 §3.2).

```
rollback(checkpoint_label) : State → State
```

7.2 Checkpoint Sources

Checkpoints are created at:

Trigger Source
Plan start A1 §5.1 (before first sub-task)
After each COMMIT A1 §3.2 (implicit checkpoint)
Explicit Checkpoint record A7 §3.2
Before sub-plan invocation A6 §4.1 (Phase 1)

7.3 Rollback Rules

Rule RB1 (Restore via AOL). Rollback reads the checkpoint from the AOL and restores state.

Rule RB2 (Rollback is an Action). Rollback is itself an action, subject to VERIFY (A5 §4.2). If VERIFY fails, rollback is refused.

Rule RB3 (Bounded Rollback Count). Rollback can occur at most R_max times per plan, a declared constant (recommended: 2).

Rule RB4 (Rollback Cycle Cost). Rollback consumes cycles. These are charged against T-AET.

Rule RB5 (No Rollback Across Plans). Rollback cannot cross a plan boundary. The checkpoint must be within the current plan.

7.4 Rollback Semantics vs. Determinism

Rollback is deterministic: given the same checkpoint, the same state is restored. It does not introduce nondeterminism.

But: rollback changes the present, which changes subsequent behavior. This is not a violation of determinism — determinism is about input sequence → output sequence, not about internal state paths.

7.5 Rollback and Logging

Rule RB6 (Rollback is Logged). Every rollback creates an AOL entry (A7 §3.2, Checkpoint record with label = "rollback").

The log records: what was rolled back, from which state, to which state, and why.

